# Coding standards

The reviewer applies these to every Python change. `scripts/check` already enforces formatting,
lint rules, docstrings, type annotations, warnings, coverage and the banned comments, so they are
not repeated here.

The reader is {{reader}}. Every rule serves that reader.

## Plain Python

Use functions, `@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)`, `enum.Enum`, `match`, `pathlib.Path`,
built-in generics such as `list[str]`, and `X | None`, plus the approved extras below.
Metaclasses, descriptors, decorators that change a signature, `__getattr__` tricks,
monkeypatching, `global` and `async` need the owner's approval first. Once approved, they go into
the list.

Approved extras:

- None.

## Comments

A reader can follow a module from top to bottom by its docstrings and comments alone.

- Every module and public function has a docstring that says what it is for and why.
- Explain a Python construct briefly the first time a module uses it, if the reader may not know
  it. For example a comprehension, a generator or `match`. One short sentence, sometimes with the
  equivalent in a language the reader knows.
- No filler, no praise of the code, no restating the code in words.

## Types and errors

- Model data with dataclasses, enums and `TypedDict`, not with `dict[str, object]` passed around.
- Expected failures are values, or specific exception types named in the docstring (`Raises:`).
  `raise ValueError("negative n")` with the message inline is fine. Never catch `Exception`.
- `json.loads` and similar APIs return `Any`, which mypy cannot check. Turn such a value into a
  typed one where it enters the program: check it with `isinstance` and build a dataclass.
- Platform-specific code goes behind one small function or Protocol that tests can replace. Then
  every branch is type-checked and covered on Linux, where CI runs.

## Functions and names

- Functions are short, do one thing and read top to bottom. Pull out a named helper instead of
  nesting.
- Names say what a value is: `environment`, `argument`. Single letters only in tiny lambdas and
  comprehensions.
- Use the same name for the same concept everywhere: in code, tests and docs.

## Dependencies

A new entry in `dependencies` in `pyproject.toml` needs the owner's approval.

## Tests

- New behaviour comes with a test. A bug fix comes with a test that was red before the fix.
- Tests are plain functions, one behaviour each, with names that describe the behaviour.
- Use a Hypothesis property test when a rule holds for all inputs.
- Every test asserts something. Coverage cannot see a test without an assertion; the reviewer can.
